\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first systematic comparison of three execution-free verification formulations (\cls{}, \cwm{}, \traj{}). At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} are statistically equivalent (TOST $p{=}0.007$, 5 seeds); \traj{} reaches parity on Qwen3 but not DeepSeek-Coder (2.69\pp{}). Per-test classification achieves 217$\times$ higher throughput while providing diagnostic signal whose downstream utility remains unconfirmed ($n{=}44$, $p{=}0.42$). Complementary errors (oracle: 90.63\%/95.83\%; single-seed) motivate complexity-aware routing. At repository level under ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning, a significant interaction ($z{=}-23.7$) confirms divergence (18--26\pp{}, confirmed across 3 seeds per model, variant-level), with \cwm{} falling below majority baseline---consistent across families but dependent on multiple confounds (\S\ref{sec:threats}). Variant-level analysis reveals three distinct failure modes (format collapse, decision collapse, aggregation penalty) consistent across model families. At function level, formulation choice is immaterial; at repository level under ${\leq}$8B LoRA, \cls{} is recommended for both accuracy and throughput.

Three directions follow. First, oracle gains (90.63\%/95.83\%) motivate complexity-aware routing among formulations; the failure mode taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:repo}) provides candidate routing features. Second, the generation bottleneck is semantic---four syntactic interventions fail (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(g))---suggesting that closing the gap requires capacity scaling or execution trace pretraining~\cite{meta2025cwm}; testing at 32B+ scale would resolve the primary open question. Third, larger-scale evaluation of per-test diagnostics for downstream repair ($n{=}44$ in our pilot) would test whether the 217$\times$ throughput advantage of \cls{} extends beyond verification to repair pipelines.

\section*{Limitations}

Repo-level \cwm{} uses 3 seeds per model (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-6.7B); the gap (18--26\pp{}) exceeds any single assessed confound (\S\ref{sec:threats}), but correlated confounds mean definitive causal attribution requires further replication. Our \cwm{} baseline uses ${\leq}$8B LoRA fine-tuning, not the original 32B CWM with execution trace mid-training~\cite{meta2025cwm}; at larger scale, the generation bottleneck may diminish. Training data comprises synthetic mutations~\cite{andrews2005mutation,just2014mutants}; the synthetic-to-real transfer gap for absolute accuracy remains untested, though the coupling effect supports relative ranking validity (Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}). Model coverage is limited to 6--8B code-specialized LLMs; generalization to larger or general-purpose models is untested. All code, data, and model checkpoints will be released upon acceptance.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Let $p$ denote a candidate patch and $T = \{t_1, \ldots, t_n\}$ its test suite, where each $t_i$ has label $y_i \in \{\text{\textsc{Pass}}, \text{\textsc{Fail}}\}$ from execution. Tests decompose into \emph{fail-to-pass} ($T_{\text{f2p}}$) and \emph{pass-to-pass} ($T_{\text{p2p}}$) subsets; a patch is correct iff $\forall\, t_i{:}\ y_i = \text{\textsc{Pass}}$. We compare three formulations for predicting correctness without execution.

\subsection{Verification Formulations}
\label{sec:formulations}

Let $c_p$ denote the code context (unified diff) of patch $p$.

\paragraph{\cls{} (per-test classification).} $f(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{y}_i \in \{\text{\textsc{Pass}}, \text{\textsc{Fail}}\}$ predicts a single binary token per test (cross-entropy loss). Patch verdict: $\hat{y}_p = \bigwedge_{i} [\hat{y}_i = \text{\textsc{Pass}}]$. Output cost: one token per test.

\paragraph{\cwm{} (output generation).} $g(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{o}_i \in \Sigma^*$ generates the execution output string (language modeling loss). Verdict by exact match: $\hat{y}_i = \text{\textsc{Pass}}$ iff $\hat{o}_i = o_i^*$.\footnote{Exact string matching is conservative, consistent with \citet{meta2025cwm}. Semantic equivalences are not counted.} When the generated output is unparseable (i.e., does not conform to the expected structured format), the prediction defaults to the majority class verdict. Output cost: $\sum_i |\hat{o}_i|$ tokens.

\paragraph{\traj{} (trajectory-level).} $h(c_p, T) \rightarrow \hat{y}_p \in \{\text{\textsc{Resolved}}, \text{\textsc{Unresolved}}\}$ predicts a single patch verdict from all tests and provides no per-test signal. \traj{} serves as a granularity control: \cls{}--\traj{} differences isolate the value of per-test decomposition independent of the classification formulation itself.

\subsection{Formulation-Specific Failure Modes}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

Each formulation has characteristic failure modes that differ in their sensitivity to code complexity:

\paragraph{Generation failure modes (\cwm{}).} The open-ended output space introduces three failure modes: (1)~\emph{token-budget exhaustion}, where $|o_i^*| > B$ guarantees exact-match failure due to truncation; (2)~\emph{string-value prediction errors}, where semantically equivalent outputs differing in surface form (floating-point precision, dictionary ordering) produce false negatives; and (3)~\emph{format compliance collapse}, where the model fails to produce structured predictions entirely at high input complexity, even when the generation budget is sufficient. Under optimistic independence, a token sequence of length $L$ with per-token accuracy $q$ has correct-sequence probability $q^L$; long outputs are therefore exponentially harder to predict exactly. We adopt exact string matching following the original \cwm{} evaluation protocol~\citep{meta2025cwm}. Unparseable predictions default to the majority-class label (\textsc{Pass}).

\paragraph{Classification failure modes (\cls{}).} The single-token output space eliminates format and truncation failures but constrains the model to encode its reasoning entirely within internal representations, without the intermediate reasoning trace that generation provides. \cls{} errors manifest as false positives or false negatives without format failures; at function level, a mild FP bias (56.5\%) emerges, with the error direction depending on class distribution and fine-tuning dynamics.

\paragraph{Trajectory-level failure modes (\traj{}).} By collapsing all per-test information into a single binary verdict, \traj{} discards diagnostic granularity. When a patch fails multiple tests, the trajectory-level formulation cannot identify \emph{which} tests fail. This limits downstream utility (e.g., targeted re-repair) but avoids per-test aggregation errors.

\paragraph{Complexity dependence.} These failure modes suggest a complexity-dependent interaction: when execution outputs are short and structured (function-level code), generation modes (1)--(2) are rarely triggered, and all formulations should perform comparably. As output length and structural complexity increase (repository-level code), generation-specific failures may compound, creating divergence. Whether this divergence reflects a fundamental property of the generation formulation or is contingent on model/tokenizer properties is an empirical question we investigate in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

\subsection{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Framework}
\label{sec:oracle}

For a buggy patch with $k = |T_{\text{f2p}}|$ fail-to-pass tests, a per-test verifier with recall $r$ detects the bug if it identifies at least one failing test: $P_{\text{detect}}(k) = 1 - (1 - r)^{k_{\text{eff}}}$, where $k_{\text{eff}} = k / (1 + (k-1)\rho)$ accounts for within-patch correlation via ICC $\rho$~\citep{shrout1979icc}. The per-test advantage over a trajectory-level baseline ($a_h$) is $\Delta(k) = P_{\text{detect}}(k) - a_h$, with population expectation $\mathbb{E}[\Delta] = \sum_{k} P(|T_{\text{f2p}}| = k) \cdot \Delta(k)$. This predicts that per-test advantage concentrates in multi-F2P-test instances and vanishes at $k{=}1$, validated in \S\ref{sec:experiments} and Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We systematically compare \cls{}, \cwm{}, and \traj{} across two representative code LLM families, two complexity levels, and multiple seeds to characterize the formulation design space for neural code verification.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
\emph{Function-level}: mutated variants from HumanEval~\cite{chen2021humaneval} and MBPP~\cite{austin2021mbpp} (five mutation types), labels verified by execution. 19,076 samples (train 15,290 / val 1,896 / test 1,890; pass:fail $\approx$ 52:48, majority baseline 51.75\%).
\emph{Repo-level}: per-test instances from SWE-bench Verified~\cite{chowdhury2024swebenchverified} mutants. ${\sim}$70K training samples; val ($n{=}9{,}335$) and test ($n{=}9{,}784$) have zero problem overlap (majority baseline 72.96\%). Val-based checkpoint selection follows standard practice~\cite{jimenez2024swebench} where validation loss is computable; runs using truncate preprocessing produce NaN validation loss due to length-mismatched sequences, which defaults to the final epoch checkpoint (affects DeepSeek \cls{} s123, all \cwm{} runs, and Qwen3 \traj{} runs; see Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparams}).

\paragraph{Models and training.}
We fine-tune Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B~\cite{qwen2025qwen3} with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 16, 3 epochs) across five seeds. Seeds 42/123/456 use bfloat16; seeds 789/0 use NormalFloat 4-bit (NF4) quantization~\cite{dettmers2024qlora} to test robustness to precision reduction (function-level accuracy remains within seed noise across both precisions; see footnote in \S\ref{sec:equivalence}). As a cross-model consistency check, we additionally fine-tune DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B~\cite{guo2024deepseekcoder} with identical LoRA configuration (2 seeds each for \cls{} and \cwm{}). Our \cwm{} baseline is a LoRA fine-tuned variant for matched comparison, not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model~\cite{meta2025cwm}. Repo-level models use val-selected checkpoints where available; runs with NaN validation loss use the final epoch checkpoint (Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparams}). Zero-shot baselines use Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-32B (NF4) as LLM-as-judge. Full hyperparameter details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparams}.

\paragraph{Statistics.}
Confidence intervals use clustered bootstrap~\cite{davison1997bootstrap} (114 problem clusters, 10K resamples; we resample entire problems, not individual tests, to preserve within-problem correlations); per-test vs.\ trajectory-level equivalence uses Two One-Sided Tests (TOST)~\cite{schuirmann1987tost} with $\pm$2 percentage point (\pp{}) margin. Holm-Bonferroni correction~\cite{holm1979} controls family-wise error rate across the four primary comparisons (Table~\ref{tab:holm}). Intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC)~\cite{shrout1979icc} quantifies within-problem correlation. Variant-level effect sizes and statistical power analysis are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:power}. Oracle analysis uses SWE-bench Verified (500 instances).


\subsection{Function-Level Results}
\label{sec:func_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports function-level results across formulations and model families.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Function-level per-test accuracy on HumanEval+MBPP ($n{=}1{,}890$). Mean $\pm$ std over seeds. Seeds 42/123/456: bfloat16; 789/0: NF4. DeepSeek: $n{=}2$ seeds; std reported for descriptive comparison, not inferential. \traj{} evaluated on 1,032-sample subset (447 matched variants).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Seeds} & \textbf{Accuracy (\%)} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\emph{Qwen3 (primary)}} \\
\cls{} (fine-tuned) & 4B & 5 & \textbf{88.43 $\pm$ 0.78} \\
\cwm{} (fine-tuned) & 4B & 5 & \underline{87.19 $\pm$ 0.37} \\
\traj{} (fine-tuned) & 4B & 5 & 86.30 $\pm$ 0.97 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\emph{DeepSeek-Coder (cross-model consistency check)}} \\
\cls{} (fine-tuned) & 6.7B & 2 & \textbf{86.85 $\pm$ 0.11} \\
\cwm{} (fine-tuned) & 6.7B & 2 & \underline{86.83 $\pm$ 1.49} \\
\traj{} (fine-tuned) & 6.7B & 2 & 84.16 $\pm$ 1.30 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\emph{Prompting baselines}} \\
0-shot & 8B (Qwen3) & 1 & 68.70 \\
0-shot & 6.7B (DeepSeek) & 1 & 63.00 \\
ZS LLM-as-judge & 8B (Qwen3) & 1 & 64.40 \\
ZS LLM-as-judge & 6.7B (DeepSeek) & 1 & 56.56 \\
ZS LLM-as-judge & 32B (NF4) & 1 & 71.69 \\
3-shot ICL & 8B (Qwen3) & 1 & 68.78 \\
3-shot ICL & 6.7B (DeepSeek) & 1 & 59.95 \\
\midrule
Majority (pass) & --- & --- & 51.75 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

All three formulations achieve comparable accuracy on Qwen3-4B: \cls{} 88.43\% ($\pm$0.78), \cwm{} 87.19\% ($\pm$0.37), \traj{} 86.30\% ($\pm$0.97). DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B shows: \cls{} 86.85\%, \cwm{} 86.83\%, \traj{} 84.16\%. The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap (0.02--1.24\pp{}) is within seed noise in both families. However, the \cls{}--\traj{} gap is model-dependent: $<$1\pp{} on Qwen3 but 2.69\pp{} on DeepSeek-Coder, exceeding the $\pm$2\pp{} equivalence margin (\S\ref{sec:equivalence}). \cls{} and \cwm{} are robustly equivalent; \traj{} parity is model-contingent. Zero-shot baselines (Tables~\ref{tab:main_results} and~\ref{tab:repo_level}) establish that repo-level verification requires fine-tuning (0-shot$\to$fine-tuned gains of 35--36\pp{}) and that formulation choice matters only at repo level (\cls{} outperforms \cwm{} by 18--26\pp{}).


\subsection{Scaling Behavior}
\label{sec:scaling}

Beyond overall accuracy, we examine whether formulation ranking is stable across model sizes. Table~\ref{tab:scaling} compares all three formulations at 4B and 8B using seeds 42 and 123 in full bfloat16 precision (excluding NF4 seeds to isolate the size effect).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Model-size scaling: accuracy (\%) at 4B and 8B (Qwen3, seeds 42/123, full bfloat16 precision). \cls{} and \traj{} scale positively; \cwm{} does not.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Size} & \textbf{s42} & \textbf{s123} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{$\Delta$ 4B$\to$8B} \\
\midrule
\cls{} & 4B & 87.83 & 89.52 & \textbf{88.68} & \multirow{2}{*}{+1.27} \\
\cls{} & 8B & \textbf{90.48} & 89.40 & \textbf{89.94} & \\
\midrule
\cwm{} & 4B & 87.51 & 87.20 & \underline{87.36} & \multirow{2}{*}{$-$0.03} \\
\cwm{} & 8B & 87.67 & 86.98 & 87.33 & \\
\midrule
\traj{} & 4B & 85.66 & 85.47 & 85.57 & \multirow{2}{*}{+2.13} \\
\traj{} & 8B & 87.60 & 87.79 & \underline{87.70} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\cls{} and \traj{} both scale positively from 4B to 8B (+1.27\pp{} and +2.13\pp{} respectively), while \cwm{} remains flat ($-$0.03\pp{}). At 8B, the ranking is \cls{} (89.94) $>$ \traj{} (87.70) $>$ \cwm{} (87.33). The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1.32\pp{} at 4B to 2.62\pp{} at 8B (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}); clustered bootstrap on the 3-seed superset (42, 123, 456 for \cls{}/\cwm{}) yields $p = 0.0066$ (95\% CI $[1.80, 2.71]$), which confirms the widening is not a sampling artifact.

\cwm{} flat scaling ($-$0.03\pp{}) may reflect suboptimal hyperparameter transfer at 8B rather than a fundamental limitation; we do not claim generation \emph{cannot} benefit from scale. At function level, outputs are short (integers, booleans) and format compliance is near-perfect; at repo level, output complexity increases substantially, and the same generation bottleneck manifests as format collapse patterns we examine in \S\ref{sec:repo}. \traj{} also shows no scaling benefit at repo level (72.92\% at both 4B and 8B); only \cls{} scales positively across both complexity levels.


\subsection{Per-Test vs.\ Trajectory-Level Equivalence}
\label{sec:equivalence}

We compare \cls{} (aggregated via all-pass rule) against \traj{} on 523 variant-level instances across five seeds.\footnote{Seeds 789 and 0 use NF4 quantized inference; the remaining three use bfloat16. Restricting to bfloat16 seeds only (3 seeds) yields $p{=}0.128$: consistent in direction but underpowered due to reduced seed coverage.} The pooled gap is $-$0.45\pp{} (95\% CI [$-$1.71, +0.84]); the TOST equivalence test confirms statistical equivalence at $p = 0.007$ ($\pm$2\pp{} margin, Holm-Bonferroni corrected). Our $\pm$2\pp{} threshold follows the SESOI framework~\cite{lakens2017}, set independently of the observed gap and grounded in deployment relevance: at function-level accuracy (85--88\%), a 2\pp{} difference corresponds to ${\sim}$38 per-test predictions across 1,890 tests (${\sim}$2--3 problems out of 114)---comparable to between-seed variation (0.37--0.97\pp{} std, Table~\ref{tab:main_results}) and checkpoint selection noise ($<$1\pp{}, \S\ref{sec:threats}). A difference within this range is indistinguishable from training noise for pipeline selection decisions. Equivalence holds at margins as tight as $\pm$1.52\pp{} (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}), well below the pre-specified threshold. Within-problem predictions are correlated (intraclass correlation ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. On DeepSeek-Coder, the observed \cls{}--\cwm{} gap of 0.02\pp{} is consistent with equivalence, but with only 2 seeds the comparison is underpowered. The DeepSeek \cls{}--\traj{} gap (2.69\pp{}) exceeds the $\pm$2\pp{} margin; \traj{} parity is model-dependent rather than universal, while the \cls{}--\cwm{} equivalence is robust across both families. Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at no cost to variant-level accuracy. This parallels process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify}: step-level verification enables error localization without accuracy loss.


\subsection{Repo-Level: Complexity-Dependent Divergence}
\label{sec:repo}

Table~\ref{tab:repo_level} compares formulations at repository-level complexity. Variant-level comparisons rely on limited seed coverage; the per-test interaction effect is statistically confirmed (see below). Checkpoints are val-selected where validation loss is computable (\S\ref{sec:setup}); the test set has zero problem overlap with validation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level cross-formulation comparison on SWE-bench Verified test set. Qwen3-8B \cls{}/\traj{}: mean $\pm$ std over 3 seeds; DeepSeek \cls{}: 2 seeds\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}; DeepSeek \traj{}: 3 seeds; Qwen3-4B: seed 42. \cwm{}: 3 seeds (42/123/456).}
\label{tab:repo_level}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{\cls{} (\%)} & \textbf{\cls{} Var.\ (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} (\%)} & \textbf{\traj{} Var.\ (\%)\textsuperscript{$\diamond$}} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-4B & 83.63 & 64.58 & 58.04 & 72.92 \\
Qwen3-8B & \textbf{84.83 $\pm$ 0.67} & 72.92 / 81.25\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 66.48 $\pm$ 1.15 & 72.91 $\pm$ 3.61 \\
DeepSeek-6.7B & 84.38 $\pm$ 2.50\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$} & 81.25\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 58.30 $\pm$ 2.82 & 77.08 $\pm$ 3.61 \\
\midrule
0-shot (Qwen3-8B) & 49.90 & --- & --- & --- \\
0-shot (DeepSeek) & 48.54 & --- & --- & --- \\
Majority & 72.96 & 54.17 & 72.96 & 54.17 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}

\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}Individual-seed variant accuracy. \textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}One seed excluded: truncate preprocessing$\to$NaN val loss, decision collapse (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}); mean over two matched seeds. \textsuperscript{$\diamond$}Var.\ columns: variant-level accuracy ($n{=}48$); \cls{} aggregated via all-pass rule; \traj{} by design. Per-test: $n{=}9{,}784$. \cwm{} unparseable: 54\% (4B); default to pass; parseable-only 38--43\%, below majority. Eval seq\_length differs across models (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}). \cwm{} uses epoch-3 checkpoint (val loss undefined); see text for checkpoint sensitivity analysis.}
\end{table}

Few-shot prompting (3-shot ICL) collapses to an always-predict-unresolved baseline (variant accuracy 45.83\%, below majority 54.17\%; Appendix~\ref{app:few_shot}).

\paragraph{Formulation divergence.} Under our 4--8B LoRA protocol, \cls{} and \cwm{} diverge on Qwen3: the gap is 25.59\pp{} at 4B (\cls{} 83.63\% vs.\ \cwm{} 58.04\%, seed 42; Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}) and 18.35\pp{} at 8B (84.83\% vs.\ 66.48\%, 3-seed means). \cwm{} falls below majority baseline at both scales. These gaps confirm complexity-dependent formulation divergence, though limited seed coverage and multiple confounds (\S\ref{sec:threats}) limit definitive causal attribution.

\paragraph{Cross-model consistency.} DeepSeek-Coder shows the same direction (\cls{} $>$ \cwm{}) across all seeds. DeepSeek \cls{} degradation ranges from ${\sim}$4\pp{} (s42, drop preprocessing) to ${\sim}$12\pp{} (s123, truncate preprocessing), reflecting preprocessing and checkpoint selection sensitivity (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap magnitude also varies across families (20\pp{} for Qwen3 vs.\ 23--28\pp{} for DeepSeek), primarily reflecting checkpoint selection: DeepSeek \cwm{}'s val-selected checkpoint (56.70\%) underperforms its epoch-3 checkpoint (61.49\%), widening the apparent gap. Under matched checkpoint selection (epoch~3 for both), the cross-model gap difference narrows to ${\sim}$2\pp{} (Qwen3: 20.47\pp{} vs.\ DeepSeek: 22.89\pp{}). The gap is formulation-driven rather than model-specific. Across all six \cwm{} repo-level seeds (three Qwen3-8B, three DeepSeek-6.7B), every seed exhibits format collapse ($>$50\% unparseable) and parseable-only accuracy below the majority baseline, ruling out seed-specific artifacts. Eval seq\_length differs between models (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}).

\paragraph{Failure mode taxonomy.} Variant-level analysis ($n{=}48$, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:variant_level}) reveals three failure modes (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}): format collapse (\cwm{}, 51--74\% unparseable), decision collapse (\cls{} under truncate preprocessing, fail recall ${\sim}$14\%), and aggregation penalty (\cls{} under drop preprocessing, fail recall 38--57\%). Decision collapse is configuration-dependent, not model-inherent. \traj{} avoids aggregation penalty by predicting variant outcomes directly (72.91--77.08\%). Downsampling \cls{} training data to match \traj{}'s data volume yields 80.74\%, still 7.82\pp{} above \traj{}; this controls data volume but not label granularity (per-test vs.\ variant-level), and remains a single-seed comparison (Appendix~\ref{app:downsample}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level failure mode taxonomy. Variant-level majority baseline: 54.17\%.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Failure Mode} & \textbf{Representative} & \textbf{Fail Recall} & \textbf{Variant Acc} \\
\midrule
Format collapse & \cwm{} (all) & N/A & 48--52\% \\
Decision collapse & \cls{} (DeepSeek, s123) & ${\sim}$14\% & 56\% \\
Aggr.\ penalty & \cls{} (Qwen3-8B) & 51--57\% & 73--81\% \\
Aggr.\ penalty & \cls{} (DeepSeek, s42) & 38--51\% & 79--81\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Default assignment artifact.} The apparent \cwm{} accuracy increase from 4B to 8B is an artifact of rising unparseable rate (53.7\%$\to$73.8\%), which inflates accuracy via majority-class default while parseable-only accuracy decreases (42.88\%$\to$38.52\%). Cross-seed evidence confirms the same artifact: DeepSeek s456 has a lower unparseable rate (57\%) yet lower overall accuracy (56.1\%) than s42 (63\% unparseable, 61.5\%), because parseable predictions (${\sim}$32\%) fall far below majority baseline (Appendix~\ref{app:parseable_bias}).

\paragraph{Complexity as moderator.} The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1--3\pp{} (function) to 18--26\pp{} (repo) under our protocol; a matched-context ablation confirms input disparity explains $<$3\% of this gap (Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\paragraph{Formulation$\times$complexity interaction.} A mixed-effects linear probability model with problem-level random intercepts ($n{=}56{,}480$ observations, 139 problem clusters, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms the interaction: the \cwm{} effect at function level is negligible ($\hat{\beta}{=}-0.002$, $p{=}0.805$), but the formulation$\times$complexity interaction is highly significant ($z{=}-23.7$, $p{<}0.001$). \cwm{} and \cls{} are statistically indistinguishable at function level ($\Delta{=}-0.2$\pp{}) but diverge by 22\pp{} at repo level, confirming the divergence is not an artifact of aggregation. The cross-complexity analysis uses DeepSeek-Coder, the only family with both function- and repo-level \cwm{} evaluations under matched conditions. Across both model families at repo level, the \cwm{} deficit is $-$22\pp{} ($z{=}-64.4$, $p{<}0.001$); the model family effect is significant but small (+1.1\pp{} for Qwen3, $p{=}0.002$), and the formulation$\times$model interaction is likewise small (+1.6\pp{}, $p{=}0.006$). Formulation explains $>$95\% of the predictable variance; model identity modulates gap magnitude without changing direction (Appendix~\ref{app:interaction}).

Additional repo-level limitations appear in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.


\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Aggregation penalty.} Under the all-pass rule, a single false negative flips the variant verdict. The penalty ranges from 1.36\pp{} (DeepSeek s42) to 19\pp{} (Qwen3-4B), depending on fail recall and test count (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:variant_level}). Val-based checkpoint selection biases toward per-test accuracy at the expense of fail recall, which amplifies the penalty. \traj{} avoids this by predicting variant outcomes directly (72.91--77.08\%). The penalty is predictable from test count: function-level variants ($\bar{T} \approx 2.3$) incur negligible penalty, while repo-level variants ($\bar{T} \gg 10$) incur substantial penalty, both the \emph{advantage} and \emph{cost} of per-test granularity concentrate at high $T$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(a)).

\paragraph{Why does generation degrade at repo level?} Output space complexity increases nonlinearly: function-level outputs are short strings (integers, booleans); repo-level outputs are unconstrained execution traces (mean ${\sim}$477 tokens) where errors compound multiplicatively, and cause format collapse (53--74\% unparseable). Even among parseable outputs, accuracy (38--43\%) is below majority baseline; forced parsing that rescues 90.6\% of unparseable outputs achieves only 49.4\% accuracy, confirming the bottleneck is semantic rather than syntactic (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(c)). An accidental training artifact---a \cwm{} model inadvertently trained on classification-format data---further corroborates this conclusion, recovering 86.05\% accuracy with 0\% unparseable outputs (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}, item~(c)). Four targeted interventions---increased LoRA capacity (rank~32), majority voting ($K{=}3$), relaxed output matching, and model scaling (4B$\to$8B)---all fail to close the gap, confirming the deficit is formulation-level rather than an artifact of any single design choice (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(g)). This extends the formulation-dependent accuracy tradeoffs documented for NLP tasks~\cite{raffel2020t5} to a regime where output complexity causes format compliance itself to become a dominant failure mode. Per-mutation accuracy breakdown and qualitative error examples are provided in Appendices~\ref{app:repo_mutation} and~\ref{app:qualitative}. \cwm{} eval loss reaches its minimum within 5\% of training and monotonically increases thereafter across all three seeds, confirming that ${\sim}$95\% of training actively increases eval loss (Appendix~\ref{app:cwm_convergence}). A DeepSeek-Coder checkpoint stopped at epoch~1 (s123) achieves 75.45\% repo-level accuracy versus 57.31\% at epoch~3 (s123; +18.14\pp{})---reducing accuracy from above the majority baseline to well below it, confirming that extended training actively degrades verification utility.

\cls{} achieves 217$\times$ higher throughput than \cwm{} (1 output token vs.\ avg 477), consistent across both model families.

\paragraph{Error complementarity.} At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} exhibit partial complementarity: both are FP-dominant (over-predict failure), but at different rates (\cls{} 56.5\%, \cwm{} 70.3\%; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(a)), so \cwm{} over-predicts failure on instances \cls{} correctly classifies. An oracle combination achieves 90.63\% (+2.80\pp{} over the best single formulation; single-seed estimate). At repo level, oracle routing across configurations reaches 95.83\% variant accuracy (+14.58\pp{} over the best single configuration, $n{=}48$), the strongest evidence that the formulation design space contains complementary solutions worth exploring. On SWE-bench Verified (500 instances), the per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}). A re-repair pilot found no benefit from per-test diagnostics ($n{=}44$, $p{=}0.42$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(j)). Extended analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}.

\subsection{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}

We identify five potential confounds and assess each in Appendix~\ref{app:threats}: checkpoint selection ($<$1\pp{} variation in ablation; NaN validation loss forces final-epoch default in all \cwm{} runs and DeepSeek \cls{} s123), evaluation sequence length (${\sim}$1.5\pp{}), preprocessing (${\sim}$8\pp{} cross-configuration variance), \traj{} training regime (downsample confirms formulation advantage), and \cwm{} scale/pretraining (primary limitation; all repo-level \cwm{} conclusions conditional on the ${\leq}$8B LoRA regime). The 18--26\pp{} gap exceeds any single assessed confound, but correlated confounds and limited seeds mean definitive causal attribution requires multi-seed replication. Holm-Bonferroni correction confirms the two core claims survive (adjusted $p{=}0.012$ and $p{=}0.021$; Table~\ref{tab:holm}).

