\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Execution Simulation and Verification.}
Execution trace generation improves code reasoning~\cite{nye2021scratchpad}, with extensions to output prediction~\cite{meta2025cwm,maimon2026selfexecution,liu2024codemind,liu2023codeexecutor,lee2026solidcoder}, failure analysis~\cite{abdollahi2025demystifying}, and error detection~\cite{li2024redo}. CRUXEval~\citep{gu2024cruxeval} benchmarks code execution prediction at function level; our work extends this direction to repo-level repair verification. Verification formulations span trajectory-level reward~\cite{shum2025swerm,zhu2026codescaler}, process-level feedback~\cite{li2025codeprm,xu2025patchreasoner}, per-test classification~\cite{ni2023lever,chen2023codet}, learned metrics~\cite{zhuo2024icescore,dong2024codescore,zhou2023codebertscore}, and RL with execution-based rewards~\cite{wang2026verpo,tang2026execverify}. Aletheia~\cite{venkatkrishna2026aletheia} compares training recipes; we compare \emph{formulations}. Test-time scaling~\cite{snell2024scaling,li2025sstar,chen2025vgsearch} and self-consistency~\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} demonstrate verification value; none compare formulations at matched capacity.

\paragraph{Process vs.\ Outcome Supervision.}
PRMs outperform ORMs for math reasoning~\cite{lightman2023letsverify,uesato2022solving}, with automated annotation~\cite{wang2024mathshepherd,luo2024omegaprm} and code-specific extensions~\cite{dreamprm2025code,ye2025prlcoder,zhang2026funprm,jiang2025coderlplus}. Our \cls{} provides per-test (process) supervision while \traj{} provides outcome-level; the PRM literature compares supervision granularity given a fixed output format, while we additionally vary the output format (\cwm{} generation vs.\ \cls{} classification).

\paragraph{Repair, Mutation Testing, and Task Framing.}
CodeRL~\cite{le2022coderl}, AlphaCode~\cite{li2022alphacode,alphacode2023team}, and Reflexion~\cite{shinn2023reflexion} use execution feedback for repair. SWE-bench~\cite{jimenez2024swebench,chowdhury2024swebenchverified} provides benchmarks; SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent} and Agentless~\cite{xia2024agentless} demonstrate pipelines where verification is a bottleneck~\cite{yang2025aprsurvey}. Predictive mutation testing~\cite{tufano2019predictive,kim2022predictive,wang2024llmmutation,jain2023contextual} shares our per-test structure. LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} and QLoRA~\cite{dettmers2024qlora} enable parameter-efficient fine-tuning. Task framing---how a problem is cast as a model's input--output mapping---systematically affects performance: \citet{raffel2020t5} showed that classification, generation, and span-extraction formulations yield different accuracy--efficiency tradeoffs across NLP tasks. We apply this paradigm to code verification, holding architecture and training data constant while varying only the formulation; our finding that complexity moderates the classification--generation tradeoff extends their analysis to a new domain.

